#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# finite_field_element

#% text
# `finite_field_element(F, k)` gives the element of the finite field `F` with index `k`.

#% text
# `finite_field_element(F, [c0, c1, ...])` gives the element `c0 + c1 x + c2 x^2 + ...` of `F`, where `x` is the generator.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `a(prop)` gives a property of the element `a`; `a([prop1, prop2, ...])` a list of them.

#% item
# The canonical form keeps the coefficients reduced modulo `p` and modulo the defining polynomial, without trailing zeros (zero is `[]`); in a field with the exponential representation it is the index (0 for zero, `k` for `x^(k - 1)`).

#% item
# The index of an element in the polynomial representation is `c0 + c1 p + c2 p^2 + ...`; an index of `q` or more is reduced like a longer coefficient list.

#% item
# The properties are those of the field (`"Characteristic"`, `"ExtensionDegree"`, `"FieldSize"`, `"FieldIrreducible"`, `"ElementRepresentation"`) and `"Field"`, `"Index"`, `"Coefficients"`, `"Trace"` and `"Norm"`.

#% item
# Elements of one field combine with each other and with exact numbers whose denominators are prime to `p`. Elements of different fields do not combine: their sum stays a sum.

#% item
# `a^k` takes any integer `k`; `a^(1/n)` stays unevaluated, `finite_field_root(a, n)` lists the roots.

#% item
# `a == b` compares elements, also with integers: `F(0) == 0` is `true`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
F = finite_field(5, 3)

#% cell
a = finite_field_element(F, [1, 2, 3])

#% cell
b = finite_field_element(F, 42)

#% cell
(a^2 + b) / (a - 3*b)
#% output
#= finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [4, 3, 2])

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Properties of an element:

#% cell
b(["Index", "Coefficients", "Trace", "Norm"])
#% output
#= [42, [2, 3, 1], 0, 1]

#% cell
a("Field") == F
#% output
#= true

#% text
# Negative powers are powers of the inverse:

#% cell
a^-1 * a
#% output
#= finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [1])

#% text
# In a field with the exponential representation, products add the indices:

#% cell
E = finite_field(5, 2, "Exponential")

#% cell
[E(4) * E(7), (E(4) * E(7))("Coefficients")]
#% output
#= [finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(2 + 4*&1 + &1^2), "Exponential"), 10), [1, 3]]

#% text
# Elements of different fields stay apart:

#% cell
finite_field(2, 2)(3) + finite_field(3, 1)(2)
#% output
#= finite_field_element(finite_field(2, &(1 + &1 + &1^2), "Polynomial"), [1, 1]) + finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(1 + &1), "Polynomial"), [2])

#% text
# Membership:

#% cell
[element(1/3, F), element(1/5, F), element(a, F)]
#% output
#= [true, false, true]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Polynomial arithmetic with coefficients in a field:

#% cell
polynomial_remainder(x^4 + a*x + 1, b*x^2 + 1, x)
#% output
#= finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [0, 4, 1]) + x*finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [1, 2, 3])

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The Frobenius map is additive:

#% cell
frobenius_automorphism(a + b) == frobenius_automorphism(a) + frobenius_automorphism(b)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# `finite_field_index` gives the index:

#% cell
finite_field_index(b)
#% output
#= 42

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Square roots by a power stay unevaluated; `finite_field_root` finds them. An unknown property too:

#% cell
[a^(1/2), a("Foo"), finite_field_root(a^2, 2) ]
#% output
#= [finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [1, 2, 3])^(1/2), finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [1, 2, 3])("Foo"), [finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [4, 3, 2]), finite_field_element(finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [1, 2, 3])]]

#% text
# A rational whose denominator is divisible by `p` is not an element:

#% cell
F([1/5, 1])
#% output
#= finite_field(5, &(3 + 3*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial")([1/5, 1])

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `finite_field`, `finite_field_index`, `finite_field_element_norm`, `finite_field_element_trace`, `frobenius_automorphism`, `finite_field_root`
